# 6. HTTP Request Structure

```
POST /login HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer TOKEN

{
   "email":"abc@gmail.com",
   "password":"123456"
}
```

An HTTP request consists of:

## Request Line

```
POST /login HTTP/1.1
```

Contains:

* HTTP Method
* URL
* HTTP Version

---

## Headers

Metadata about the request.

Examples:

```
Host
Authorization
Content-Type
Accept
User-Agent
Cookie
Content-Length
```

---

## Body

Contains the actual data.

Example:

```json
{
  "email":"abc@gmail.com",
  "password":"123456"
}
```

Used mainly with:

* POST
* PUT
* PATCH

---

# 7. HTTP Response Structure

Example:

```
HTTP/1.1 200 OK

Content-Type: application/json

{
   "message":"Login Successful",
   "accessToken":"xxxxx"
}
```

Contains:

* Status Line
* Response Headers
* Response Body

---

# 8. HTTP Methods

## GET

Retrieve data.

```
GET /products
```

Should not modify server data.

---

## POST

Create a new resource.

```
POST /products
```

Body:

```json
{
  "name":"Laptop"
}
```

---

## PUT

Replace an entire resource.

```
PUT /products/1
```

---

## PATCH

Update only selected fields.

```
PATCH /products/1

{
   "price":45000
}
```

---

## DELETE

Delete a resource.

```
DELETE /products/1
```

---

# 9. Common HTTP Headers

### Host

Identifies the target server.

```
Host: api.example.com
```

---

### Content-Type

Specifies the body format.

```
Content-Type: application/json
```

---

### Accept

Specifies what response formats the client accepts.

```
Accept: application/json
```

---

### Authorization

Contains authentication credentials.

```
Authorization: Bearer JWT_TOKEN
```

---

### Cookie

Sends session information.

---

### User-Agent

Identifies the client application.

